WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
extraordinary developments in  afghanistan the u.s says it's sending  some 3 000 troops to help evacuate  embassy staff in kabul the decision  comes with the beleaguered afghan  government struggling to turn the tide  against a relentless taliban offensive  the uk is also sending soldiers to help  british nationals leave the country the  taliban are on the march across  afghanistan and appear to have taken  control of herat the country's third  largest city that comes hot on the heels  of militants over running the strategic  town of ghazni just 150 kilometers south  from the capital kabul heavy fighting is  also taking place in kandahar the  country's second biggest city the us  believes the afghan capital itself could  come under attack by the taliban within  weeks and is now asking its citizens to  leave the country immediately  the taliban triumphant in ghazni just  one of a string of provincial capitals  to fall to the militants  they've met barely any resistance it  seems that without backing from us and  other international troops afghan  government forces are reluctant to make  a stand  our security forces are still fighting  the opposition in some areas  but most of ghazni province is under the  total control of the taliban enemy  ghazni lies on a vital highway  connecting the major cities of mazari  sharif in the north and kandahar in the  south both already under heavy attack by  the taliban  and just a short distance from ghazni is  the capital kabul itself  we remain optimistic but we were always  concerned that if the central and local  governments did not pay special  attention to ghazni and it fell  we would see a huge security challenge  threatening kabul  now ghazni has fallen giving the  militants a route straight to kabul and  wherever they take ground they usually  find stockpiles of weapons and vehicles  left behind by fleeing government troops  this video appears to show the taliban  releasing army soldiers who surrendered  further north in kunduz  the militants claim the troops then  joined their ranks but neither the  images nor the information can be  verified  there is no doubt however that the  militants are rapidly gaining ground  and they may yet prove unstoppable  well let's talk to bradley bowman he's  with the center on military and  political power at the foundation for  defence of democracies he joins us from  washington welcome to the program a  string of provincial capitals are being  captured employees from the u.s embassy  in kabul have now been told to leave  are you surprised at the sheer speed at  which this is now unfolding  thanks for the opportunity to join you  and thanks for the question it's  certainly a devastating catastrophe that  we are witnessing unfold in afghanistan  many of us have warned the bite  administration against implementing a  timeline-based conditions ignoring  withdrawal  based on this idea of endless wars that  we've heard so many pundits and  politicians push for so long this is  what you get when you do a conditions  ignoring withdrawal we've seen 12  provincial capitals fall in seven days  more than one-third of afghanistan's  provincial capitals have fallen within a  week  more vulnerable than ever  our national security and my view should  be based on facts on the ground and our  enemies don't stop fighting just because  we withdraw and and what we're seeing  right now is a vivid and sad example of  this the victims in the short term are  going to be afghans particularly afghan  women and girls we're all going to be  victims in the long run because we know  that problems in afghanistan don't stay  there  as the regional cities started to fall  you were quoted earlier this week as  saying the situation in afghanistan was  a predictable disaster what do you now  predict is going to happen in kabul  your predictions are dangerous in  afghanistan but uh you know i would have  if you'd asked me months ago  you know i i warned by administration  not to proceed with after the april 14th  announcement they've done so anyways i  would have predicted that you would have  seen uh significant taliban gains in the  south and the west based on traditional  strengths there the big surprise has  been the gains in the north the large  majority of the provincial capitals that  have fallen have been in the north and  the reason why that's significant for  your viewers is that's the traditional  political strength strong points if you  will of the kabul government badakshan  and other areas like that are either  controlled or contested by the taliban  so clearly the taliban is trying to  isolate the kabul government and  overthrow it and they've been pursuing  this rural strategy that that frankly  the department of defense kind of  ignored for too long and they've now  surrounded the cities and and they're  going into action meanwhile the biden is  ignoring this and withdrawing uh and uh  and and and the really we should be  honest is the us-led nato withdrawal  that has catalyzed what we're seeing now  and it's absolutely sad and tragic  and with that said u.s president joe  biden has doubled down on his decision  saying afghans need to quote fight for  their nation  they've received training from american  and german troops equipment too over the  course of 20 years what more could have  been done to protect them  we had 3 500 troops in afghanistan give  or take in april 3  500 american troops with roughly double  that number of nato troops for 3 500  troops we had we were preventing another  911 attack we were preserving the gains  that afghan women and girls have made  and we were preventing massive new  refugee flows that were going to  destabilize nuclear and pakistan we had  26 000 american troops on capitol hill  after january 6. 3 500 troops was not an  unreasonable investment despite that  biden doubled down on his mistake in the  obama ministration from 2011 in iraq  they conducted a conditions ignoring  withdrawal there and then we got we got  isis and had to go back in 2014. he's  repeating that exact same mistake now in  afghanistan and i fear we're all going  to pay a price  bradley bowman senior director of the  center on military and political power  at the foundation for defence of  democracies thanks for your time  thank you  well the relentless advance of the  taliban has forced thousands of afghans  to flee many have sought safety in the  capital kabul while others are hoping to  leave the country entirely  they fled the taliban's brutal advance  since may more than 220 000 afghans have  been forced to abandon their homes and  seek shelter in other parts of the  country  others are desperate to get out  at the border with pakistan afghans are  waiting for a crossing to reopen  it's just one of many barriers they face  at this point we're primarily worried  that afghans are actually going to get  stuck in afghanistan if taliban controls  border crossings for example if  neighboring countries are effectively  shutting their borders like for example  turkey did towards iran and of course  also the eu has really strengthened  their fortress as we've always already  called it fortress europe and they  really made sure  that they work with their partners who  then in order try to block their borders  and which really then keeps people  unfortunately in an area they are not  safe in  the situation in afghanistan has  prompted some to raise the specter of  the summer of 2015 when germany took in  a million people seeking refuge  mostly from war-torn syria  but a repeat of those events is unlikely  after pressure from eu ambassadors in  afghanistan germany has now suspended  deportations of rejected afghan asylum  seekers  but only temporarily  germany and europe's new focus lies in  keeping those fleeing from getting to  europe by supporting third-party  countries who will host them  that position is unlikely to change as  olaf schultz chancellor candidate for  the social democrats told us  many countries aren't governed the same  way we would expect in germany but they  do offer protection that's why there  need to be prospects for integration in  those countries in africa asia and south  america and we need to share  responsibility  one group that the german government has  vowed to help are afghans who worked  with german troops stationed in the  country and who now face acute danger  but despite promises the process of  getting them to safety has been slow  and as the taliban capture more and more  territory many of them may not get out  before it's too late  you

